    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d313">

                <pb facs="0274" n="264" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg264"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4–2249: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister-Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Clark</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canton</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-04-22T15:00:00+08:00">April 22, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 22—8:20 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Cantel 251. Wu Tieh-cheng,<anchor n="34" xml:id="d313-f1"/> just returned from
                    Nanking via Chikow where he saw Generalissimo,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d313-f2"/>
                    tells me Generalissimo and Acting <pb facs="0275" n="265" xml:id="pg265"/>
                        President<anchor n="36" xml:id="d313-f3"/> lunching together today Hangchow
                    to plan future. He insists Generalissimo continues support Li fullest. Among
                    decisions to be reached Hangchow is that of selecting over-all military
                    Commander in Chief. Wu thinks Ho Ying-chin<anchor n="37" xml:id="d313-f4"/> will
                    be selected, possibly with Pai Chung-hsi<anchor n="38" xml:id="d313-f5"/> as
                    deputy.</p>
                <p>Wu says harshness Communist terms has united all forces opposing Communists and
                    has renewed and strengthened will to resist. “We will stop them everywhere,” he
                    said.</p>
                <p>All government organs not already [in] Canton, including Cabinet and Legislative
                    Yuan, will move here immediately. Acting President expected possibly next
                    week.</p>
                <p>Despite forceful manner in which Wu expressed determination stop Communists, he
                    admitted they had already crossed Yangtze in two places—one west of Wuhu, the
                    other up river from Chinkiang, at both of which places serious fighting, he
                    said, is now taking place.</p>
                <p>Repeated Nanking 210, Shanghai 161.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Clark</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d313-f1" n="34" xml:id="d313-f1-content">
                <p>Former Vice President of the Chinese Executive Yuan and Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d313-f2" n="35" xml:id="d313-f2-content">
                <p>Chiang Kai-shek, who retired as President on January 21, 1949.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d313-f3" n="36" xml:id="d313-f3-content">
                <p>General Li Tsung-jen.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d313-f4" n="37" xml:id="d313-f4-content">
                <p>President of the Chinese Executive Yuan (premier).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d313-f5" n="38" xml:id="d313-f5-content">
                <p>Military and Political Affairs Director for Central China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
